### Three problems in one payload, so the claim is that the refusal names EVERY one of them rather
### than the first. §0 principle 4's reader is an agent, and a 422 reporting one problem per round
### trip costs it one request per mistake.
###
###   title   - 201 characters where the field declares maxLength 200
###   status  - 'urgent' is not one of the three declared enum values
###   colour  - a key the entity does not declare at all
# @name ManyProblems
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [422]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/todos
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx",
  "status": "urgent",
  "colour": "red"
}

### An empty body: both required fields are absent, and both are named.
# @name NothingSent
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [422]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/todos
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### The two optional fields really are optional — the minimum viable todo is a title and a status.
# @name MinimumViable
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/todos
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "{{runToken}}-minimal", "status": "todo" }

### After two refused writes, nothing of theirs was stored. Scoped to this run's rows so a second
### `--test-only` measures its own writes rather than the union with the last run's.
# @name NothingWasWritten
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/todos?title=like.{{runToken}}-minimal%25&order=title.asc&limit=100
